Chapter: Lz4Hostile
  cites Compress chapter Lz4

 Track D item 19, the Lz4 half. Every read in the decompressor was a bare
 list-at, which traps out of range, so a literal count, a match offset or a
 match length off the stream decided where a read landed and a hostile one
 halted the machine. The existing test is a round trip through our own
 compressor, which can only produce well-formed streams, so nothing in the
 tree had ever handed this decoder a byte it did not write itself.

 Reached, behind a hash: archive-expand (apps/works/FactArchive.codex:177)
 decompresses and then compares fa-hash-bytes against the base record's
 digest, so a truncated answer is refused by the caller. That is why every
 guard here CLAMPS -- stops and returns what was decoded -- rather than
 growing a refusal channel the caller already has.

 Rows 1 to 4 are the positive controls: real streams from lz4-compress,
 which must round trip exactly as before. Rows 5 to 8 are the hostile
 streams. Rows 5, 6 and 7 kill the guest without the guards; row 8 does not
 crash unguarded and is the discriminator, because the old safe-src clamped
 a negative source to index 0 and FABRICATED bytes that were never in the
 stream.

Section: Helpers

 Every row computes inside the print statement rather than in a let above the
 act block, so a row that dies names itself by the rows that printed before
 it. gguf-hostile paid for this lesson.

  list-equal : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  list-equal (a) (b) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then True
    else if list-at a i == list-at b i then list-equal a b (i + 1) len
    else False

  check-rt : List Integer, Text -> Text
  check-rt (input) (label) =
    let compressed = lz4-compress input
    in let decompressed = lz4-decompress compressed
    in let ok = if list-length decompressed == list-length input then list-equal input decompressed 0 (list-length input) else False
    in label & "=" & if ok then "pass" else "fail"

  make-repeated : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  make-repeated (val) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else make-repeated val n (i + 1) (list-push acc val)

 A hostile row reports the LENGTH it decoded and the first byte, which is
 what separates "stopped where it should" from "invented bytes".

  show-decode : List Integer, Text -> Text
  show-decode (input) (label) =
    let out = lz4-decompress input
    in label & " len=" & show (list-length out) & " first=" & (if list-length out == 0 then "none" else show (list-at out 0))

Section: Hostile Streams

 Row 5. Token F0 asks for a 15+ byte literal run and the extra-length byte
 says 20, so the run is 35 bytes long against a 2-byte stream. Unguarded,
 lz4-copy-literals reads input[2] and the guest dies. Guarded, the literal
 copy stops at the end of the input and the offset read that follows does
 not fit either, so nothing is decoded.

  lit-past-end : List Integer = [240, 20]

 Row 6. The offset is a two-byte field and the old guard only asked whether
 its FIRST byte was inside the stream. Here after-lit is exactly len-1, so
 the old test passed and the read of after-lit+1 went off the end.

  offset-straddles-end : List Integer = [16, 65, 0]

 Row 7. A back-reference with offset 0. Unguarded, src is list-length acc
 and list-at acc (list-length acc) is out of range by exactly one.

  offset-zero : List Integer = [16, 65, 0, 0]

 Row 8. A back-reference reaching 5 bytes behind a 1-byte output. This one
 never trapped: safe-src clamped the negative source to 0 and the decoder
 answered five copies of a byte the stream never encoded.

  offset-before-start : List Integer = [16, 65, 5, 0]

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (check-rt [] "1-empty")
    print-line-uni (check-rt [1, 2, 3] "2-tiny")
    print-line-uni (check-rt [65, 66, 67, 68, 65, 66, 67, 68, 65, 66, 67, 68] "3-repeating")
    print-line-uni (check-rt (make-repeated 42 100 0 []) "4-uniform-100")
    print-line-uni (show-decode lit-past-end "5-lit-past-end")
    print-line-uni (show-decode offset-straddles-end "6-offset-straddles-end")
    print-line-uni (show-decode offset-zero "7-offset-zero")
    print-line-uni (show-decode offset-before-start "8-offset-before-start")
  end

